<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albertus Magnus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1200–1280</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Albert the Great, Saint Albert</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albertus Magnus was a German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friar</a>, philosopher and scientist, teacher of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a>, who did much to bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s works into Christian learning. He served briefly as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Regensburg from 1260 to 1262 before resigning to return to teaching. He was canonised in 1931 and named a Doctor of the Church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albertus-magnus.jpg" alt="Albertus Magnus" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vicente Salvador Gómez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vicente_salvador_gomez-san_alberto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Albertus Magnus was probably born before 1200, since well-attested evidence shows he was over 80 when he died in 1280. Two later sources give his age as about 87, which led to 1193 being commonly given as his birth year, but that information cannot be confirmed. He was probably born in Lauingen, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, since he called himself Albert of Lauingen. He was probably educated chiefly at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, where he was taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s writings. In 1223 (or 1229) he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican Order</a> and studied theology at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> and elsewhere.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Albert taught for several years as lecturer at the Dominican house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, and also in Regensburg, Freiburg, Strasbourg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a>. In 1245 he became master of theology under Guerric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-quentin">Saint-Quentin</a>, the first German Dominican to do so, and taught at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> began to study under him. He was the first to comment on virtually all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s writings, which led him to the teachings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Averroes</a>. In 1254 he became provincial of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican Order</a>. At the General Chapter at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valenciennes">Valenciennes</a> in 1259 he helped set up a program of studies that made philosophy part of Dominican training.</p><p>In 1260 Pope Alexander IV made him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Regensburg, an office he resigned within a few years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-iv">Pope Urban IV</a> then relieved him of his duties as bishop and asked him to preach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighth-crusade">Eighth Crusade</a> in German-speaking countries. He then became known as a mediator, and in 1258 his verdict, the Große Schied, ended a conflict between the citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and the archbishop.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Albert's writings, collected in 1899, filled thirty-eight volumes, on topics from logic and theology to botany, astronomy, mineralogy and zoology. He believed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s approach to natural philosophy posed no obstacle to a Christian view of the natural order. His books on botany, zoology and minerals drew on ancient sources and on his own empirical investigations. In embryology it has been claimed that little of value was written after Aristotle until Albert, who identified organs within eggs. Many alchemical works were later falsely attributed to him, and there is scant evidence that he performed alchemical experiments himself.</p><p>Albert died on 15 November 1280 in the Dominican convent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. His relics are in a Roman sarcophagus in the crypt of St. Andrew's Church there. He was beatified in 1622, canonised and proclaimed a Doctor of the Church on 16 December 1931 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-xi">Pope Pius XI</a>, and named patron saint of natural scientists in 1941. His feast day is 15 November.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albertus Magnus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its philosophy rests on Aristotle, known to Dante from Latin translations and through <b>Albertus Magnus</b>, and on Scholasticism, especially Thomas Aquinas, so that the poem has been called “the Summa in verse”.</blockquote><a href="/work/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The scholar <b>Albertus Magnus</b> served briefly as bishop in 1260 to 1262.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-regensburg">Prince-Bishopric of Regensburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lauingen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.57,10.43&amp;year=1252" aria-label="Lauingen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4792 466 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Albertus Magnus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5242 736h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5242 736h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.57,10.43&amp;year=1252">What was here in 1252? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
